# Modal Routing Pattern

Modals are **routed** (URL-driven), not only opened with local React state. Next.js parallel routes replace Angular’s auxiliary `modal` outlet.

**Routed modals are for create / edit forms only.** A list page's filters + results render on the page route, never in a modal (see `nextjs-list-pages.mdc`).

## App Router shape

```
src/app/examples/{area}/
  layout.tsx                 # renders {children} and {modal}
  page.tsx                   # the list page: filters + grid inline (no list modal)
  @modal/
    default.tsx              # null default
    (.)manage/item/.../page.tsx  # intercepting route → create / edit form modal
    (.)manage/item/.../loading.tsx  # RoutedModalLoadingFallback (same title / tid / size; renders as `${tid}-fallback`)
  manage/
    item/.../page.tsx        # full-page fallback: list page + form modal on top
```

`layout.tsx` must accept and render the `modal` slot:

```tsx
export default function Layout({ children, modal }: { children: ReactNode; modal: ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <>
      {children}
      {modal}
    </>
  );
}
```

## Opening & closing

- Open: `useModalNavigate().open(href, pending)` — always pass `pending` ({ title, tid, size, level, closeLabel } matching the target `RoutedModal`) so the modal opens on the click (see below). Plain `router.push` gives no instant shell.
- Close: `useModalDismiss(dismissHref, preferBackOnClose)` or `RoutedModal`’s built-in dismiss
- Keep href builders in `shared/utils/{feature}-routes.ts`

## Modal component

Feature modals wrap content in `RoutedModal` from `@design-system`:

- `dismissHref` — route to return to (the list page route)
- `size` / `level` — match stacking (form directly over the page → `primary`; only a modal stacked over another modal → `secondary`)
- `presentation` via `modalProps` when a blade is required

Confirm dialogs can use `ConfirmModal` without a dedicated route when they are transient.

## Open immediately, load inside (Unity form-modal loader)

A form modal **never waits for its schema / record before appearing**. Same as Unity UI's `app-form-modal-template [loading]`:

1. **On click** — `useModalNavigate().open(href, pending)` shows `PendingRoutedModalHost` (mounted once in `app/layout.tsx`): modal shell + `CompanyLoader`, before Next.js has the route (dev compile, RSC payload, client chunk).
2. **Route loading** — `loading.tsx` in the intercepted segment renders `RoutedModalLoadingFallback` (same title / tid / size) while the form chunk loads; in production prefetch this shows instantly. Its dialog tid is `${tid}-fallback`.
3. **Form mounted** — `RoutedModal` clears the pending shell in a layout effect (one commit, no flash). Pass `loading={form.schemaLoading || !form.formVm}`; `useJsonSchemaEntityForm` starts with `schemaLoading: true`, so the first paint is the loader, never an empty body.
4. **Loaded** — `ModalShell` drops the overlay and shows the form (children stay mounted under the loader so data effects run).

- The loader is `CompanyLoader` from `@design-system` (port of Unity `app-company-loader`) over `--app-loader-backdrop-bg-strong`; the body keeps `min-height: 40rem` while loading. Don't hand-roll spinners in feature modals.
- Buttons keep their own in-flight spinner (`Button loading`), not `CompanyLoader`.
- `Modal` uses a shared body scroll-lock counter, so overlapping modals (pending → real, confirm over form) never leave the page locked or unlocked.
- **One element per tid.** `tid` is on the real `RoutedModal` dialog panel only. The shell inside it is `${tid}-shell`, the pending shell `${tid}-pending` (its shell `${tid}-pending-shell`), the `loading.tsx` fallback `${tid}-fallback`, and the loader overlay while the form loads `${tid}-loading`. Tests wait for `tid` and for `${tid}-loading` to be gone before typing.
- Browser Back / Forward while the pending shell is up clears it (`PendingRoutedModalHost` listens for `popstate` and clears when the pathname leaves both the opener and the modal href).
- Early typing is kept: `useJsonSchemaEntityForm` drops a load that finishes after a newer one started (Strict Mode double mount, reopen), and `JsonSchemaForm` ignores the parent handing back older form data, so a quick Name then Date Born is never wiped.
## Fast first open (preload)

The page that opens a form modal warms it with `useModalPreload` from `@design-system` — route prefetch plus the form module and its schema — so the first open is as quick as later ones:

```tsx
useModalPreload(animalFormHref(), () =>
  import('@json-schema-form').then(({ prefetchSystemJsonSchema }) =>
    prefetchSystemJsonSchema(apiRoutes.animals.createAnimalSchema)
  )
);
```

- `fetchSystemJsonSchema` caches schemas (5 min, cleared on auth cleared), so repeat opens skip the schema request.
- Grid row actions with `prefetchHref` are prefetched by `EntityGrid` on hover / focus of the action — always set `prefetchHref` on Edit-style actions.
- Do not `next/dynamic` / lazy-load `RoutedModal`, `ModalShell` or the form component; that re-introduces the first-open delay.
- `next dev` compiles each route on first visit and skips prefetch, so the very first open in dev is slower than production. `npm run dev:turbo` (Turbopack) shortens it.

